<!--
  What a refutation looks like in this format, with illustrative figures.

  The model makes claims that could be wrong, and this document files two
  counter-examples rather than arguing them, beside a third case worth seeing:

    1. A layer with no quantum and no premium. `object-storage` is bought continuously,
       at the same unit price as under any commitment, and commits no nameplate, so its
       remainder is `notApplicable`: there is nothing for demand to come off. If a stack
       were full of these, the model would have no subject.

    2. A coupling. A stack is assumed to be a set of layers whose remainders move
       independently, and the other filings have not tested that. A filed coupling says
       two layers' remainders moved together, which is the model's own falsifier, and it
       has to carry what was observed.

    3. A `transition` fit. `compute` demands 11.0, 13.2 and 16.4 GPU against a nameplate
       of 16, so it is short at the top of its range and spare at the bottom. The
       remainder's quantity cannot say both, and the fit does.

  A document with no couplings is not evidence of independence. It is a document where
  nobody looked. This one looked.

  It carries no operations at all: a stack is a complete document.
-->
<pm:processModulus xmlns:pm="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0">

  <!-- Which filing this is: the URN a part or a dependence cites to point at this
       document. -->
  <pm:notation>
    <pm:uri>urn:example:filing:acme-platform:2026-Q2-refutation</pm:uri>
  </pm:notation>
  <pm:evidence>
    <pm:attests>observation</pm:attests>
  </pm:evidence>

  <!-- Two regimes for one entity, which is correct and not a duplicate. The same
       Portuguese microentity is `NC-ME` to the IES and `M` to the SAF-T. The two
       published lists of four values do not line up: NCRF and NCRF-PE both fall into
       `S`, and `O` has no AnexoASNC counterpart, so neither code can be derived from
       the other, and neither declaration says what the pair says.
       The taxonomy URIs are placeholders; a real document names the real ones. -->
  <pm:regime>
    <pm:id>ies-anexo-asnc</pm:id>
    <pm:jurisdiction>PT</pm:jurisdiction>
    <pm:framework>
      <pm:term>
        <pm:taxonomy>urn:example:pt:ies:anexo-asnc</pm:taxonomy>
        <pm:value>NC-ME</pm:value>
      </pm:term>
    </pm:framework>
    <!-- The same chart as `saft-referencial` below. A chart is national, and the
         authority that codes the framework is a separate axis from it. -->
    <pm:chart>
      <pm:term>
        <pm:taxonomy>urn:example:pt:snc:chart</pm:taxonomy>
        <pm:value>SNC</pm:value>
      </pm:term>
    </pm:chart>
  </pm:regime>

  <pm:regime>
    <pm:id>saft-referencial</pm:id>
    <pm:jurisdiction>PT</pm:jurisdiction>
    <pm:framework>
      <pm:term>
        <pm:taxonomy>urn:example:pt:saft:referencial</pm:taxonomy>
        <pm:value>M</pm:value>
      </pm:term>
    </pm:framework>
    <!-- The same chart again: only the authority that codes the framework differs. -->
    <pm:chart>
      <pm:term>
        <pm:taxonomy>urn:example:pt:snc:chart</pm:taxonomy>
        <pm:value>SNC</pm:value>
      </pm:term>
    </pm:chart>
    <pm:note>The same entity and the same framework as ies-anexo-asnc, coded by a
             different authority. The SAF-T list is coarser, so this code cannot be
             mapped back to a single AnexoASNC value.</pm:note>
  </pm:regime>

  <pm:stack>
    <pm:scope>
      <pm:scope>
        <pm:extent>unbounded</pm:extent>
        <pm:basis>the three layers this filing needs: the two the coupling observation runs between, and one bought continuously with no nameplate. What else exists was not the question this filing put</pm:basis>
      </pm:scope>
    </pm:scope>

    <!-- ============================================================
         1. THE COUNTER-EXAMPLE: a supply with no quantum.
