                  </pm:narrowsWhen>
                  <pm:boundOrigin>
                    <pm:absent>
                      <pm:reason>none</pm:reason>
                      <pm:note>nothing sets this bound. It is how much of the remainder this holder was observed to take</pm:note>
                    </pm:absent>
                  </pm:boundOrigin>
                </pm:claim>
              </pm:share>
            </pm:holder>
          </pm:holder>
          <!-- The second holder follows from this supply's own capacity slack. That slack is
               a measured zero, since a reserved card cannot be run above its rating at any
               price, so the 0.4 cannot have been absorbed: there is no overtime for
               silicon. Work that could not be absorbed and could not be served went
               unserved, and unserved demand is `customer` or `unrealised`. `customer` here,
               because the request was made and somebody was on the other end of it;
               `unrealised` would mean it never reached anybody at all. "Refused" would be
               accurate on this layer, since a reserved card errors the call, but the pair
               of holders also covers the caller who waited and left, so the model uses the
               neutral word (see `Fit`).
               The share is `unmeasured`, and that is the honest filing: nobody counts
               inference requests that were never served. One unmeasured share suspends the
               rule that the shares add up to the remainder, rather than breaking it, and
               that is right here too, because under a `transition` fit the two sides move
               in opposite directions, and adding them would report a week that never
               happened. What is known without counting anything is the ceiling, 0.4 GPU. -->
          <pm:holder>
            <pm:holder>
              <pm:kind>customer</pm:kind>
              <pm:share>
                <pm:absent>
                  <pm:reason>unmeasured</pm:reason>
                  <pm:note>a request that went unserved at the top of the demand range leaves no record: it is a failed call, not a transaction. The exposure can be worked out and the count cannot: at most 0.4 GPU, the 16.4 at the top of the demand range less the nameplate of 16</pm:note>
                </pm:absent>
              </pm:share>
            </pm:holder>
          </pm:holder>
          <pm:quantity>
            <pm:claim>
              <pm:low>0.0</pm:low><pm:mostLikely>2.8</pm:mostLikely><pm:high>5.0</pm:high>
              <pm:unit>GPU</pm:unit>
              <pm:denominator>
                <pm:absent>
                  <pm:reason>notApplicable</pm:reason>
                </pm:absent>
              </pm:denominator>
              <pm:narrowsWhen>
                <pm:absent>
                  <pm:reason>unmeasured</pm:reason>
                  <pm:note>nobody has said what would tighten this range</pm:note>
                </pm:absent>
              </pm:narrowsWhen>
              <pm:boundOrigin>
                <pm:derivation>
                  <pm:identity>magnitude</pm:identity>
                  <pm:note>the bounds follow from this layer's demand and nameplate, whose own origins are stated there</pm:note>
                </pm:derivation>
              </pm:boundOrigin>
            </pm:claim>
          </pm:quantity>
        </pm:remainder>
      </pm:remainder>
    </pm:layer>

    <pm:layer>
      <pm:name>labour</pm:name>
      <pm:demand>
        <pm:amount>
